<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The release build talks to its server over https:// only (#202).

  This file used to permit cleartext to every host, on the premise that a
  school's server would be uvicorn on a machine in the building, reached by a
  LAN address no public CA will certify. What crossed such a server in the
  clear, readable by anyone on the same school Wi-Fi, was:
    - the class bearer, frequently a WRITE token since personal connect codes
      exist;
    - the diary bearer that opens the child's marks and attendance through us;
    - the diary session the phone registers with our server at
      POST /api/v1/diary/session - a working credential for the child's diary
      until it expires.
  The owner decided on 27 September 2026 that this is not worth the LAN case: a
  school server on plain http:// stops working in the release APK, and that is
  accepted.

  Two hosts are still allowed cleartext, because a request to either never
  leaves the phone: localhost and 127.0.0.1 - a server running on the phone
  itself, or one reached through `adb reverse`.

  The debug build keeps cleartext for every host, for development against a
  server on the same desk: src/debug/res/xml/network_security_config.xml has
  the same name, and a build type's resources override main's. Nothing in
  Gradle chooses between them.

  The app asks this file before it sends anything (CleartextPolicy in
  :core:data, through NetworkSecurityPolicy - the same question OkHttp asks),
  so an http:// address is refused where it is typed and before any request,
  with a sentence about https rather than a network error. NetworkSecurityConfigTest
  in :app holds both files to the rule above.

  The diary's password is not affected either way: it goes only to the diary's
  own https origin, from the catalog bundled with the app, and never to this
  server.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <!-- System CAs only. User-installed CAs stay untrusted, so adding a
                 certificate to the device cannot silently intercept the
                 server's https://. -->
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
    <!-- The trust anchors are the base config's: a domain-config inherits what
         it does not set. -->
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
